  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1256 AM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nToday through Tue night...A shortwave ejecting from the eastern\nPacific upper level low will push the ridging from yesterday\nwill into eastern MT and spread some light precipitation across\nthe central Idaho mountains and the northern half of the\nforecast area mainly. A second, weaker, shortwave arrives at the\nend of Sun night and lasts through Mon, with even lighter \nprecipitation. Mon night transitions to a stronger upper level\nridge, pumped up by a deeping upper level low off the OR-WA\ncoast. The produces a dry and clear-sky Tue. This upper level\nridge just starts to break down a little on late Tue night,\nbringing light precipition--maybe--to the western portion of the\ncentral Idaho mountains. \n\nA shift to warmer southwesterly flow should warm temperatures a\nlittle, mainly to the southern half of the forecast area where\nthe skies will be clearer. Incoming cloud cover on Sun night\nwill not help temperatures from being even colder than Sat night.\nWidespread cloud cover and the colder start on Mon should mean \na slight drop in afternoon highs, then the colder Mon afternoon\nleads to the coldest night of the period. The clear skies of Tue\nleads to a slight warming of the afternoon highs from Mon.\n\nNo significant wind expected during this near term period.\n\nWed through Sat...The deeper south to southwest flow will bring\nin a warmer and potentially wetter air mass. The best chance for\nprecipitation from this event is Thanksgiving Day, when that\nupper level ridge is broken down solidly by a second shortwave.\nAt least, digital output from guidance says this. However, the\nclusters indicate strong 500mb ridging dominates, with, maybe\nsome overruning, but no troughing. A brief upper level ridge\nleaves Fri dry, then it breaks down with the most solid trough\nof the clusters moving in for the weekend. Air flow is mostly\nzonal, though, so not a major impact to the temperatures, which\nhave warmed up to maybe above normal by Sat. High degree of\nuncertainty for this entire extended period. The only trend that\nlooks solid is warmer temperatures with the dearth of a\nnortherly component to prevailing wind and steering flow.",
